# Cybersecurity Fundamentals: The CIA Triad and the Threat Landscape

> Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting computers, networks, and
> data from unauthorised access, attack, and damage. This lesson covers
> the CIA triad, the malware family tree, and social engineering.

## 1. Assets, Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Risk

Every security conversation starts with four words that students
routinely confuse. Fix the vocabulary first and the rest follows.

| Term | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Asset | anything valuable enough to protect | customer database, laptop, reputation |
| Threat | anything that could harm an asset | ransomware gang, disgruntled insider |
| Vulnerability | a weakness a threat can exploit | unpatched server, weak password |
| Risk | likelihood times impact of a threat exploiting a vulnerability | "phishing is common and our staff untrained" |

[definition]
Risk management is the continuous process of identifying assets,
listing threats and vulnerabilities, estimating risk, and then
reducing risk with controls to an acceptable level.
[/definition]

A control is any safeguard — technical, procedural, or physical — that
reduces risk. Firewalls, backup policies, and security training are
all controls aimed at different parts of the same equation.

## 2. The CIA Triad

The classic model of security goals has three corners:

| Goal | Question it answers | Typical controls |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Confidentiality | only the right people can read | encryption, access control, classification |
| Integrity | data is not altered unnoticed | hashing, checksums, digital signatures |
| Availability | data and services work when needed | backups, redundancy, DDoS protection |

[definition]
The CIA triad — Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability — is the
three-part yardstick for security goals. Every control you will ever
meet protects one or more of these corners.
[/definition]

[warning]
Encryption only delivers confidentiality. An encrypted file that a
malware process silently corrupts is confidential but has lost
integrity, and an encrypted server that is offline has lost
availability. Never answer "encryption" for every CIA question —
match the goal to the control.
[/warning]

## 3. Beyond CIA: Authentication, Authorisation, Non-Repudiation

Three supporting goals complete the picture, often written as AAA.

- **Authentication** — proving *who* you are: passwords, OTPs,
  biometrics, security keys.
- **Authorisation** — deciding *what* an authenticated identity may
  do: file permissions, role-based access.
- **Accountability** — being able to trace actions back to an
  identity: audit logs.
- **Non-repudiation** — a sender cannot later deny having sent or
  signed something, usually achieved with digital signatures.

[tip]
Exam trap: authentication is about identity, authorisation is about
permission. A user can authenticate successfully and still be
forbidden to open a file — that is authorisation doing its job.
[/tip]

[example]
Alice signs a contract with her private key and Bob verifies it with
her public key. The signature proves integrity (nothing changed),
authenticates Alice, and gives non-repudiation — she cannot plausibly
deny signing, because only her private key could have produced it.
[/example]

## 4. Malware: The Family Tree

**Malware** is any software written to harm or misuse a system. The
families are best remembered by two questions: *how does it spread*
and *what does it do*?

@diagram tree id="malware-family-tree" layout="tree" aria="Major malware families grouped by spread mechanism and payload"
  "Malware" -> "Virus — needs a host file"
  "Malware" -> "Worm — spreads by itself"
  "Malware" -> "Trojan — hides in useful software"
  "Malware" -> "Ransomware — encrypts and extorts"
  "Malware" -> "Spyware — watches and records"
  "Malware" -> "Adware — forces ads and trackers"
@/diagram

| Family | How it spreads | What it does | Notable example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Virus | attaches to a host file, runs when the host runs | corrupts files, spreads on sharing | Michelangelo, 1992 |
| Worm | copies itself across networks unaided | consumes bandwidth, drops payloads | Morris worm, 1988 |
| Trojan | masquerades as useful software | opens backdoors, installs other malware | Emotet |
| Ransomware | via phishing, worms, or exposed services | encrypts data, demands payment | WannaCry, 2017 |
| Spyware | bundled downloads, drive-by installs | records keystrokes, exfiltrates data | Pegasus |
| Adware | free apps and bundles | floods ads, tracks behaviour | bundled browser toolbars |

[info title="The worm that started it all"]
The Morris worm of 1988 infected roughly ten percent of the early
internet and led to the first conviction under the US Computer Fraud
and Abuse Act. It spread by exploiting known flaws plus weak
passwords — a playbook still used today.
[/info]

## 5. Ransomware and Spyware Up Close

Ransomware deserves special attention because it now dominates
headlines. Modern strains use **double extortion**: first they steal a
copy of the data, then encrypt it, then demand payment to unlock — and
a second payment to keep the stolen copy private.

[warning]
Paying the ransom is discouraged: it funds crime, marks you as a
paying customer, and guarantees nothing. The controls that actually
work are patched software, least-privilege accounts, and backups that
are offline or immutable so the malware cannot encrypt them too.
[/warning]

Spyware and **keyloggers** quietly capture what you type and where you
click. Anti-spyware defences mirror the basics: download software only
from official stores, watch browser extensions, and treat an unusually
hot or busy device as a hint that something is watching.

## 6. Social Engineering: Hacking Humans

The cheapest way past a firewall is a person. **Social engineering**
manipulates psychology — urgency, authority, fear, curiosity — rather
than technology.

| Technique | Channel | Signature move |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Phishing | mass email | fake bank login page |
| Spear phishing | targeted email | references your real project or boss |
| Vishing | voice call | fake "bank fraud department" |
| Smishing | SMS | "parcel held, pay a small fee" |
| Pretexting | any channel | invented persona and scenario |
| Baiting | physical or download | infected USB labelled "salary list" |
| Tailgating | office door | follows a badged employee inside |
| Quid pro quo | call or message | fake IT help in exchange for credentials |

[example]
In business email compromise, an attacker registers a lookalike
domain — say, rnicrosoft.com — and emails the accounts team as the
"CEO", demanding an urgent transfer. No malware, no exploit: pure
pretexting with authority and urgency.
[/example]

[tip]
Three red flags cover most phishing: **urgency** ("act within 24
hours"), **authority** ("the CEO demands this"), and **secrecy**
("do not tell anyone"). When all three appear together, verify
through a known independent channel before doing anything.
[/tip]

## 7. Measuring the Threat Landscape

Real breach data keeps repeating one lesson: the human element —
phishing, stolen credentials, and simple error — features in the
majority of incidents, ahead of exotic zero-days.

@chart donut title="Approximate root causes of data breaches (illustrative)" labels:
Phishing and social engineering
Stolen or weak credentials
Malware and ransomware
Unpatched software vulnerabilities
Insider misuse and simple error

data:
30
25
20
15
10
@/chart

Another landscape term worth knowing is the **APT** — Advanced
Persistent Threat. An APT is a well-resourced group, often
state-linked, that stays inside a network for months, moving quietly
toward a specific goal rather than smashing and grabbing.

## 8. Principles That Scale

Four timeless principles turn the vocabulary above into practice:

| Principle | Rule of thumb |
| --- | --- |
| Least privilege | give every account only the access it needs |
| Defence in depth | stack controls so one failure is not fatal |
| Separation of duties | no single person can both create and approve |
| Keep it patched and backed up | close known holes; rehearse restoration |

For backups, remember the **3-2-1 rule**: three copies of important
data, on two different media, with one copy off-site — and at least
one copy unreachable by the network that ransomware walks through.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | Remember this |
| --- | --- |
| Risk | threat meets vulnerability on an asset |
| CIA triad | confidentiality, integrity, availability |
| Non-repudiation | digital signatures stop later denial |
| Virus vs worm | virus needs a host, worm self-spreads |
| Trojan | malicious logic disguised as useful software |
| Ransomware | encrypts, extorts; double extortion steals first |
| Spyware | silent observation, keylogging |
| Social engineering | urgency, authority, secrecy are the tells |
| Vishing vs smishing | voice call vs SMS phishing |
| 3-2-1 rule | three copies, two media, one off-site |
